        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The historical foundation",
        "content": "Iran’s National Heritage Protection Act of 1930 established an inventory-based approach to protection. The English translation held by UNESCO describes an original scope extending to movable and immovable heritage predating the end of the Zand dynasty. It required an inventory of significant known heritage and the addition of newly discovered items. These provisions explain the register’s historical foundation; they are not a complete statement of present-day eligibility or procedure. [s1]\n\nThe text also distinguishes registration from acquisition. It provides for notification and resolution of an owner’s objection before private-property registration becomes final, and states that registered property may remain in private ownership or possession. Registration therefore should not automatically be described as nationalization. These are provisions of the historical text inspected here, not individualized advice about a contemporary property dispute. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "expanded-eligibility",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why age alone does not explain the register",
        "content": "A separate National Heritage Registration Act widened the scope for immovable properties. Its single article authorized registration of properties significant for history or national importance regardless of age and origin, subject to the approval mechanism specified in that law. It also placed those properties under the national heritage rules. Consequently, the original Zand-era threshold cannot be used on its own to conclude that a later building is ineligible. [s2]\n\nThe translation records approval by the National Consultative Assembly on 23 October 1973 and passage by the Senate on 3 December 1973. These are legislative milestones, not registration dates for individual monuments. Its references to the Ministry of Culture and Art and the Supreme Council of Culture and Art belong to that historical administrative setting; they do not identify the office to which a present-day applicant should submit a file. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "documentation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A designation depends on evidence",
        "content": "A joint UNESCO–ICOMOS monitoring report on Bam describes registration documentation including measured drawings and accounts of a property’s architectural, artistic, or structural features. This helps explain why a register is more than a list of attractive old buildings: the decision depends on identifying and documenting significance. The report is a historical account of administrative practice, not a verified current application checklist. [s3]\n\nDocumentation also provides a basis for recognizing what could be lost through alteration. For a building, a name and approximate age alone reveal little about its fabric, layout, or distinctive construction. The report’s description supports the importance of recording those characteristics, while leaving open how current dossiers are assessed and updated. A reader checking an individual designation should therefore seek the actual registration record rather than infer legal scope from a tourism description. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "protection-and-outcomes",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Registration and conservation outcomes",
        "content": "Registration establishes a protective designation; whether a place is maintained successfully is a further question. UNESCO’s legal database brings together separate Iranian instruments dealing with registration, institutional responsibilities, excavation, and damage to cultural properties. Reading one historical act in isolation cannot resolve every contemporary permission, enforcement, or ownership question. [s4]\n\nThe central distinction is between evidence that a property has been recognized and evidence about what happens afterward. This page establishes the historical basis and expansion of registration. Current submission routes, objection procedures, and the legal position of particular properties require further verification before this account can serve as a present-day procedural guide. [s1][s2][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "legislative-development",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "Two documented legislative milestones",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"1930 protection act\"] -->|\"Defines original scope\"| B[\"Heritage predating the end of the Zand dynasty\"]\n  A -->|\"Requires\"| C[\"Inventory of significant heritage\"]\n  D[\"1973 registration act\"] -->|\"Extends eligibility\"| E[\"Significant immovable properties regardless of age\"]\n  E -->|\"Remain subject to\"| F[\"National heritage rules\"]\n```",
